WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.625
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:05.430
I help some tax experts gain
performance independence.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.590
If their friends' performance
jumped off a cliff,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.522
would they do, too?

00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:09.480
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.480
Tax Lemming is a startup focused
on making sense of purchases,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.455
taxes, and basic bookkeeping
for your small business needs.

00:00:19.455 --> 00:00:21.360
But they had a problem
that was keeping them

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.559
from going for their
next round of VC funding.

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:25.350
They've got too many
instances spinning up,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.040
and it was breaking the bank.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.210
See, in a rush to put
together a working prototype,

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.300
the Tax Lemming's client
hit a Google Load Balancer,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.840
which fetched content from a
GC instance running Apache.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.660
Now to improve performance,
their lead engineer

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.910
wanted to move to a very
traditional solution

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.100
for web-based
applications-- adding

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.910
a layer of NGINX instances to
act as a reverse caching proxy

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.790
to the Apache servers,
and then change the client

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.490
to fetch static content
directly from a CDN instead

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.930
of the back end instances.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.770
Now this is a very
tried and true solution.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.520
But before they started
tearing things up,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.040
I wanted to present them
with an alternative way

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.230
to get the same caching
performance but with less

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:09.060
instances and lower costs
using GCP's Load Balancers.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.060
See, one secret that GCP
Load Balancers have is that

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.305
with the click of a button--

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:16.320
that button-- the Load
Balancer will cache requests

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.780
and move them to
Google Cloud CDN

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.850
to reduce latency
on your behalf.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.920
As long as the
request is cached,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.290
it will be served directly
at Google's Network Edge

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.180
rather than going
to the back end.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.700
Performance-wise,
this is a huge boost.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.520
We can easily see the difference
when comparing direct access

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.160
to the resource through the
instance versus allowing

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.050
the load balancer to
cache the request.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.750
This graph shows that once
the asset gets cached,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.760
the response time
drops significantly.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.520
And all you had to
do was check a box.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.920
What's even better about this
is that the cached content

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.800
is served right from
Google CDN without any need

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.260
for caching instances to exist.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:58.020
To use a new buzzword, you
could call this "serverless."

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.050
I'm cool.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.500
Now, another nifty feature
that the load balancers

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.290
allow you to do is
create host routing paths

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.750
for your domain, which
will serve content directly

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.729
from a Google Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.590
This way you don't have
to modify the client

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.777
to fetch new types of content
from a different CDN location.

00:02:13.777 --> 00:02:15.360
Instead, you can
just treat the assets

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.980
as though they
were subdirectories

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.264
and the load balancers
will do the right thing.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:21.180
And of course, to improve
performance further,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.850
you can even cache those
assets to the CDN, as well.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.910
So with a few button
clicks, Tax Lemmings

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.620
was able to cut down
their instance count

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.999
and reduce their maintenance
headaches long-term.

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:32.790
If you'd like more
details on this problem,

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.740
check out the full article
linked in the description

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.240
below.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.740
And make sure to subscribe
to our Google Cloud YouTube

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.690
channel to get alerts when new
performance videos comes out.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.730
And don't forget, when
it comes to performance,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.020
every millisecond counts.

